    "title": "What to Know About Nowruz, the Persian New Year",
    "text": "AdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENTNowruz, the Persian New Year, Celebrates Renewal and RebirthMore than 300 million people in Iran and beyond observe the holiday, though the celebrations this year have been muted because of the U.S.-Israeli war with Iran. Here’s what to know.Listen · 4:49 min VideoHappy Nowruz, the Persian New YearIn 2025, nearly a year before the start of the U.S.-Israeli war with Iran, Persians around the world celebrated with dance and fire, and shopped for holiday essentials as they prepared for the start of a new year.CreditCredit...Ahmed Gaber for The New York TimesMarch 20, 2026Nowruz, the Persian New Year, falls on Friday, but what is normally a festive celebration of life, renewal and rebirth has taken on a more subdued tone this year as the U.S.-Israeli war with Iran prepares to enter its fourth week.More than 300 million people worldwide observe Nowruz, which means “new day” in Persian. Many of them greet one another by saying “Nowruzetan pirooz” or “Wishing you a victorious Nowruz!”The holiday, which is tied to the spring equinox, traces its roots to the Persian Empire. People in the Balkans, the Black Sea basin, the Caucasus, Central Asia and the Middle East have celebrated it for more than 3,000 years.In a normal year, the buildup to Nowruz begins “as soon as it starts to warm up a little bit in late February and the beginning of March,” said Alireza Hedayati, president of the Iranian American Society of New York, a nonprofit organization based on Long Island.“Everyone just gets that little bit of an excitement and the urge to do things,” Mr. Hedayati said.This year, however, the holiday has come around at one of the most challenging times for Iranians in recent memory. Widespread economic pain touched off protests late last year that broadened into a mass movement challenging Iran’s authoritarian clerical rulers, who responded with a brutal crackdown. Then, on Feb. 28, the U.S.-Israeli bombardment began in Iran.Here’s what to know about one of world’s oldest holidays:Holiday CustomsIn the lead-up to the new year, Nowruz celebrants thoroughly clean their homes. Mr. Hedayati likened it to spring cleaning.Subscribe to The Times to read as many articles as you like.AdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENT",
